  "findings": [
    "Both raw reports have exactly 8 goals, 6 passed. Both byte_step ensures time out; wrong_roundtrip ensures is unknown in raw01 and timeout in typed01. All four assigns pass; direct roundtrip and typed_step ensures pass. Exit 0 is not all-goals acceptance.",
    "MemTyped.ml:927-928 uses addr_of_int(v) and Cint.of_integer(iota,int_of_addr(l)); integer conversion can truncate. MemAddr.ml:300-307 simplifies int_of_addr(addr_of_int(a)) to a and zero conversions. The simplifier direction is integer-to-address-to-integer, not the direct pointer-to-integer-to-pointer control.",
    "cil.ml:5744-5753 removes direct nested pointer-to-integer-to-pointer casts when can_hold_ptr; this explains the direct-control success without demonstrating a general WP address bridge. README wording one kind of roundtrip is technically compatible but should specify this direction and distinguish CIL elimination.",
    "memaddr.mlw:137-145 declares abstract conversions, null conversion, and a statically_allocated pointer inverse; there is no direct linear int_of_addr/shift equation in the inspected physical-address section. These are pre-existing model axioms, not new additions.",
    "README explicitly avoids concluding impossibility, a real counterexample, or a repaired general bridge from timeout/unknown. That limitation is correct.",
    "The byte_step requires prevent multiplication overflow and demand a readable typed range, but do not establish a universal ISO C integer-address arithmetic law or explicitly bound uintptr base-plus-offset. This is an implementation/model-specific diagnostic obligation; no recommendation to add kernel preconditions or an unproved bridge axiom."
  ],
  "suggested_readme_clarification": "Specify MemAddr integer→address→integer simplification versus CIL direct pointer→integer→pointer elimination, and say the offset equality needs an explicit implementation/model contract rather than being an unconditional ISO C guarantee."
